Decision for the maintainer: options and a recommendation, not decided here.

The plugin starter downloads the release binary once and runs it from then on, at every SessionStart and every UserPromptSubmit.

1. Trust anchor for the checksum.
   (a) Checksums pinned in the plugin. The release writes them into the plugin after the binary build: one line per platform, for version X. The plugin a user installs then names the exact bytes it will run.
       Cost: a two-step release (tag vX, build, commit the checksums, marketplace entry at that commit). Without reproducible builds the tag itself does not contain them.
   (b) SHA256SUMS fetched from the same GitHub release. This checks integrity only: whoever can replace the release assets can replace both files. GitHub's immutable releases make that harder.
   (c) (b) plus an artifact attestation check. It needs `gh` on the user's machine, so no.
   Recommendation: (a). A hook that downloads and runs a binary on every machine should run only bytes the installed plugin commit names.

2. Where the binary lives.
   (a) $CLAUDE_PLUGIN_DATA. Claude Code's per-plugin data dir; winlaunch knows it as {data}. But Codex and Hermes start the same bin/skilletor and may not set it.
   (b) <claudeConfigDir>/skilletor/bin/<version>/, beside the state and the cache, shared by every harness.
   (c) Next to the starter in the plugin dir. Replaced on every plugin update, and possibly read-only.
   Recommendation: (b). Old versions are swept like the cache.

